{"person_id":"robert-morris","name":"Robert Tappan Morris","cohort_group":"Researchers / independent engineers","category":"Computer scientist","birth_year":1965,"age_at_milestone":22,"milestone_by_age_26":"Created and released the Morris worm on November 2, 1988, the first Internet worm, which infected approximately 10% of internet-connected computers and became a landmark event in computer security history.","early_history_summary":"Morris grew up in a household deeply immersed in computing; his father Robert Morris Sr. was a Bell Labs researcher who helped design Unix and later became chief scientist at the NSA's National Computer Security Center. Morris attended Harvard for his BA, then began graduate studies at Cornell, where he developed and released the Morris worm in November 1988 at age 22. By his teens he was already contributing patches to BSD Unix.","primary_leverage_engine":"Technical depth","normalized_primary_engine":"Scarce technical / intellectual depth","secondary_engine":"Family-domain apprenticeship","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":1,"prior_reps_score":2,"scarce_skill_depth_score":2,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":2,"complementary_team_score":0,"structural_wave_score":1,"concentration_intensity_score":2,"capital_safety_score":1,"domain_proximity_score":2,"total_leverage_score":13,"success_tier":2,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"High","family_context_summary":"Born to Robert Morris Sr., a Bell Labs computer scientist who helped design Unix and later became chief scientist at the NSA's National Computer Security Center, and Anne Farlow Morris. Grew up in a household deeply immersed in computing and computer security.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Father was a Unix developer at Bell Labs (1960-1986) and chief scientist at the NSA's National Computer Security Center (1986-1994), providing direct domain expertise in the same field as the milestone.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":1,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":2,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":2,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":1,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":1,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":1,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":0,"early_online_platform_community_score":0,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":1,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":1,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":0,"inherited_access_stack_count":7,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":2,"all_documented_early_condition_count":7,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Family-domain apprenticeship","primary_early_advantage_tags":"Unix household, Bell Labs father, NSA chief scientist, Harvard, early computing access, BSD patches","evidence_summary":"Morris grew up in an exceptionally privileged computing environment: his father was one of the original Unix developers at Bell Labs and later chief scientist at the NSA's computer security center. This gave Morris rare early exposure to Unix internals and security vulnerabilities. By his teens he was contributing patches to BSD Unix. At age 22, as a Cornell graduate student, he released the Morris worm, the first Internet worm, which became a landmark event in computer security. His family-domain advantage was directly and unusually catalytic to his milestone.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"High","source_count":5,"primary_source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_T._Morris","source_urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_T._Morris","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Morris_(cryptographer)","https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/technology/30morris.html","https://phreak.fm/signals/robert-morris-worm-second-act","https://www.csail.mit.edu/person/robert-morris"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.3.0-trajectory-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible","starting_point":"Born to Robert Morris Sr., a Bell Labs computer scientist who co-developed Unix, and Anne Farlow Morris; grew up in an elite computing household with direct access to Unix systems and security expertise.","current_position":"Professor of computer science at MIT CSAIL; retired co-founder of Y Combinator; resides in Cambridge, Massachusetts.","current_position_year":2025,"is_living":true,"trajectory":[{"year":1988,"age":22,"event":"Released the Morris worm from MIT, the first Internet worm, infecting approximately 10% of internet-connected computers"},{"year":1990,"age":25,"event":"First person convicted under the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act; sentenced to 3 years probation, 400 hours community service, and $10,050 fine"},{"year":1995,"age":30,"event":"Co-founded Viaweb with Paul Graham, one of the first software-as-a-service companies"},{"year":1998,"age":33,"event":"Viaweb acquired by Yahoo for approximately $49 million in stock, rebranded as Yahoo Store"},{"year":2005,"age":40,"event":"Co-founded Y Combinator with Paul Graham, Jessica Livingston, and Trevor Blackwell"},{"year":2006,"age":41,"event":"Received tenure at MIT in the Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science"},{"year":2019,"age":54,"event":"Elected to the National Academy of Engineering"}]}
